ctually prefer  to do it as we do it at link not  surprisingly and that is to look up the  individual words  personally i find the translation or the  bilingual text distracting  but other people love doing it that way  so you know this is not this is good  this is bad it's a matter of what works  for you individually i like the idea  that  you know it's much like a link when we  start a new  uh language all the words are  highlighted in blue  as i go through and pick out the words  that i need to learn they convert to  yellow and then i can get rid of all the  other blue words in that text  convert them to white and once a word is  converted to yellow or white whenever  that word appears again it'll be yellow  or white so then i can focus more and  more on the words that i don't know  uh  once i have saved a word then i can  review it in flashcards i don't do a lot  of flashcarding but when i do i'm  looking for  i'm looking to train my brain to the  relationships between words the  components that reappear in different  words and so very often when i do  flashcards i have the  the foreign word and the english  translation  and the phrase that we capture all on  the front i'm just looking at them i'm  going through quickly i don't want to  challenge my brain to try to remember it  personally i don't find that that works  for me what works for me is exposure so  i review them quickly and try to see if  i can train my brain to see  the connections between different words  uh when i read at link because the words  that i previously saved are highlighted  in yellow i find that that helps to  remind me that i've looked it up of  course i can see the meaning and so it's  just this constant exposure and all of a  sudden lo and behold  words that i couldn't remember and  couldn't remember i see them in more and  more different contexts  i start to notice them now the more  language learning is a bit like a jigsaw  puzzle the more of the pieces you have  in place  the more  new things that used to just go flying  by you that you didn't notice now you  start to notice now another thing that  helps you notice obviously is to read  some description of the frequently  occurring patterns in the language or a  grammatical explanation but i've also  found that  a grammatical explanation that i read  at an early stage  it doesn't have much impact on me but at  a later stage once i have already  kind of seen that pattern then if i read  the grammatical explanation it helps me  notice it again  and words that i have tried to remember  i'll try to  uh or that i've worked in my flash cards  on or that i know that these are  important words for me when i hear them  then i try to make a mental note that  i've i've heard them  i always like to listen to whatever i  read and read whatever i listen to  because i find that that reinforcing  also helps me notice things  with regard to pronunciation we have to  be very careful not to allow  our native language  and particularly the writing system in  our native language to influence  how we what we hear  so if we are  uh say a spanish speaker where the  phonetic  uh you know the alphabet is very  phonetical  and so every word always has the same  um uh sound in spanish so if we come to  english and we see a word like word  w-o-r-d word  bird b-i-r-d  heard  h-e-a-r-d we have to start noticing that  it doesn't matter how those words are  written in fact they're all pronounced  the same so we need that deliberate  attempt to start noticing these kinds of  things and so you train yourself to  notice you get better the more you're  into the language and of course the more  languages you know  the easier it is to notice because you  are already familiar with similar  patterns in other languages particularly  if there are related languages so it's  easier to notice  how they might differ in the new  language or or how they might be the  same so it's this this old  adage the sufi sufist adage i often  mention this you can only learn what you  already know but really what that means  is  you know once you notice something or  maybe it's pointed out to you then you  have a better chance of noticing it the  next time and the more you notice things  the more likely it is it's event that  the more likely it is to eventually  stick so to me language learning is a  matter of noticing and frequently  noticing and that in this way eventually  things stick in the brain  much more so than deliberately trying to  memorize something which i have found  not so effective  uh yeah mistakes here's another one when  you speak or write when you are  corrected to me  if i am corrected or if i correct  someone else i don't expect that that  person that i corrected or the person  who corrected me will enable me to get  it right the next time correction is not  about getting it right the next time  correction is about  making you start to notice and so by  pointing it out  i know yeah yeah but i'll still get it  wrong the next time but if it's pointed  out again and maybe i start to notice  uh when i'm listening  slowly  there's a chance that my brain will  start to pick up on this because the  brain is trying to create rules and  establish patterns so that it can deal  with this you know new experience which  is the new new  language so there are these things that  you can do  to help the brain  let's see i made some notes i don't  always make some notes here to help the  brain  notice all right so there you have it i  mean it's it's it's individual it's the  key  uh  learning skill  noticing  because you have to condition your brain  to the new language once you have these  words in you that they're part of that  system then to actually  develop the skill of speaking and  writing and understanding is going to  flow from that because if you've noticed  all these things and you've built up the  vocabulary and you've noticed  these different structures and you've  noticed the pronunciation so they become  really  a part of how you use the language it's  now starting to fall into place now if  you want to develop the skill to speak  better you have all the elements in  place so my advice is  focus on deliberately noticing  be concerned about your ability to  notice  don't get upset if you read a rule  grammar rule and forget it if you are  corrected and make the same mistake  again  if you study a list of words and can't  remember them  if you're reading in the same places are  still difficult to understand none of  that matters because the sheer fact of  doing those things is gradually and  deliberate is  unmistakably going to enable your brain  to start noticing these things  and your task is to expose the brain to  enough stimulus both in the form of the  language and some of these other  activities that it starts to notice and  once it starts to notice it'll build for  you a whole control center for the new  language so just to summarize the three  here you've got to be positive about the  language you have to like the language i  think your emotional relationship to the  language is extremely important to  learning it you've got to be confident  about your own ability  uh  you know you got to like the task you  don't want to get frustrated at yourself  all of these  attitude factors are extremely important  the second thing was time it takes time  the brain learns all the time but the  brain learns slowly so if you are  serious about learning the language you  have to put in the time and put the time  in enjoyably and efficiently in whatever  activities you find enjoyable and  efficient for you and then the third  issue was this developing the ability to  notice initially through just a lot of  exposure a lot of listening and reading  and speaking  and then  taking advantage of when a mistake is  pointed out  or you're skimming through a grammar  book or you're flipping through some  flash cards  realize that these things are helping  you to notice and be a little bit  deliberate in that you want to notice  when you're listening oh yeah there was  that or when you're reading like i'll  often underline a phrase because i know  i consistently get it wrong and all of  this is sending signals to your brain  that's going to help your brain notice  not only the details of the language but  put together sort of a comprehensive  control center for the language  so there you have it i think that  language learning is essentially quite a  simple concept  those are the three elements your  attitude  the ability to enjoy your commitment  your connection to the language the time  you put in and then developing this  ability to notice so i look forward to  any questions bye for now
 

